CONSTRUCTION CONTRACT

THIS CONSTRUCTION CONTRACT ("Agreement") is entered into effective as of May 19, 2025 ("Effective Date"), by and between:

OWNER: Harborview Capitol Tower LLC, an affiliate of Harborview Realty Partners, LP, a California limited partnership, located at 500 Capitol Mall, Suite 1800, Sacramento, California 95814 ("Owner")

and

CONTRACTOR: Meridian Mechanical Group, Inc., a Delaware corporation doing business as Meridian Plumbing, Heating & Air, located at 2150 Harbor Point Drive, Suite 400, Sacramento, California 95833 ("Contractor")

RECITALS

WHEREAS, Owner owns and operates Capitol Tower, a twenty-five-story commercial office building located at 1020 J Street, Sacramento, California (the "Project"); and

WHEREAS, Owner desires to engage Contractor to perform certain mechanical work at the Project involving replacement of obsolete chiller and piping systems; and

WHEREAS, Contractor desires to perform such work under the terms and conditions set forth in this Agreement.

NOW, THEREFORE, in consideration of the mutual covenants and agreements contained herein, and for other good and valuable consideration, the receipt and sufficiency of which are hereby acknowledged, Owner and Contractor agree as follows:

1. THE WORK

1.1 Scope of Work. Contractor shall provide all labor, materials, equipment, services, and temporary facilities necessary to complete the following scope of work at the Project (collectively, the "Work"):

(a) Complete removal and proper disposal of two (2) existing 400-ton capacity centrifugal chilling units located in the basement chiller plant, including disconnection of all refrigerant piping, condenser water connections, electrical service, and vibration isolation supports;

(b) Delivery to the Project site, installation, and startup of two (2) new 400-ton capacity centrifugal chilling units, including all associated electrical connections, control wiring, condenser water piping connections, refrigerant charging and startup, and manufacturer's commissioning services;

(c) Complete removal and proper disposal of two (2) existing cooling towers located on the rooftop of Capitol Tower, including disconnection of supply and return piping, electrical disconnect, and removal of support structures;

(d) Delivery to the Project site, installation, and startup of two (2) new cooling towers with capacity sufficient to handle the heat rejection requirements of the new chiller system, including all associated supply and return piping, electrical service, and performance balancing;

(e) Removal, replacement, and installation of all building-wide copper condenser water piping running from the chiller plant in the basement to all air handling units, fan coil units, and terminal equipment throughout floors 1 through 25 of Capitol Tower, utilizing Type K copper tubing with sweat or compression fittings, including insulation, supports, and vibration isolation where required by code;

(f) Removal, replacement, and installation of all domestic hot and cold water piping systems serving the Capitol Tower building, including main supply lines from the building entrance, distribution piping to all floors, branch connections to all lavatories, kitchenettes, and mechanical spaces, utilizing Type K copper tubing with all appropriate fittings and pipe insulation in compliance with California Plumbing Code;

(g) Performance of pressure testing of all replaced piping in accordance with the specifications, including hydrostatic testing at 1.5 times the working pressure with documentation of results;

(h) System flushing, cleaning, and commissioning of all chiller, cooling tower, and piping systems in accordance with ASHRAE guidelines, including balancing of water flow rates and verification of proper system operation;

(i) Full startup, testing, and training on all new equipment for Owner's maintenance and operations personnel, including preparation of operations manuals and as-built documentation;

(j) Temporary support of building water and cooling systems during the transition period from old to new equipment to ensure continuous service to all building occupants;

(k) Site cleanup and removal of all job site waste, packaging materials, and removed equipment from the Project and proper disposal at appropriately licensed facilities; and

(l) Final restoration of the chiller plant, rooftop, and all affected areas to a clean, finished condition.

1.2 Contract Documents. The Work shall be performed in accordance with the Contract Documents, which consist of the following and are mutually incorporated into this Agreement:

(a) This Agreement and all exhibits attached hereto;

(b) Mechanical Design Drawings prepared by Morton & Associates Mechanical Engineers, dated March 15, 2025, consisting of 12 sheets numbered M-1.0 through M-12.0, showing chiller plant details, cooling tower installation, and complete piping routing;

(c) Mechanical Specifications for Chiller and Piping Replacement prepared by Morton & Associates, dated March 15, 2025, including Division 23 HVAC Systems specifications;

(d) All Addenda issued prior to the Effective Date of this Agreement;

(e) All properly executed Change Orders;

(f) Any Purchase Orders issued in connection with the Project; and

(g) All applicable amendments or modifications agreed to in writing by the parties.

In the event of any conflict or ambiguity among the Contract Documents, the following order of precedence shall control: (1) this Agreement, (2) Change Orders (with most recent Change Order controlling), (3) Specifications, (4) Drawings, and (5) Addenda.

1.3 Standards of Performance and Compliance. Contractor shall execute all Work in a professional and workmanlike manner consistent with industry standards for mechanical contracting. All Work shall comply with the 2022 California Building Code, 2022 California Plumbing Code, 2022 California Mechanical Code, current ASHRAE standards, and all applicable local ordinances and amendments adopted by the City of Sacramento and Sacramento County. All workers performing mechanical work shall hold valid California C-36 (Plumbing) or C-20 (HVAC) contractor licenses or shall work under the direct supervision of a licensed contractor. All mechanics shall hold valid certification as required by California law. Contractor warrants that all materials supplied shall be new, of good quality, free from manufacturing defects, and in full compliance with the specifications and codes.

1.4 Site Examination and Pre-Construction Inspection. Contractor represents that it has thoroughly examined and inspected the Project site, the Capitol Tower building structure, the existing chiller plant equipment room and its access points, the rooftop location for cooling towers, existing piping routing and conditions, the occupied building environment, and all areas where Work will be performed. Contractor's examination has included investigation of: (i) the existing chiller plant location, size, structural capacity, headroom, and equipment arrangement; (ii) the rooftop cooling tower location, structural capacity, and access for new equipment installation; (iii) the complete routing of existing condenser and domestic water piping throughout the 25-story building; (iv) the condition and dimensions of building risers and spaces available for new piping; (v) the coordination requirements with ongoing building operations and active tenants; (vi) anticipated weather conditions in the Sacramento area during performance; (vii) availability and cost of qualified labor for the specialized chiller and piping work; (viii) costs of delivery and installation of large mechanical equipment; (ix) coordination requirements with other building trades and vendors; and (x) all site constraints and logistical considerations. Contractor has compared all site conditions with the Drawings and Specifications and has satisfied itself regarding the feasibility of the Work. Contractor confirms that it is fully satisfied with site conditions and that the Contract Sum reflects all costs necessary for proper and complete performance of the Work under the existing site conditions. No allowance shall be made to Contractor for any error, omission, or failure to examine site conditions except to the extent that actual conditions differ materially from those indicated in the Contract Documents or reasonably apparent from inspection, and then only to the extent permitted by Section 5.3.

1.5 Pre-Construction Meeting and Schedule. Within 7 days of the Notice to Proceed, Contractor shall attend a pre-construction conference with Owner's representatives to discuss scheduling, site logistics, building coordination, phasing of equipment removal and installation, emergency procedures, and communication protocols. Contractor shall provide Owner with a detailed construction schedule within 14 days of the Notice to Proceed showing all phases of work, with particular emphasis on: (a) the sequence and timing of existing chiller removal to minimize disruption to building climate control; (b) the schedule for new chiller delivery, uncrating, rigging, and installation into the basement; (c) the timing of new cooling tower delivery and rooftop installation; (d) the complete schedule for replacement of condenser and domestic water piping by floor and zone; and (e) the testing, commissioning, and startup timeline. The schedule shall identify all critical path items and shall demonstrate how Contractor will maintain building services throughout the transition.

2. CONTRACT SUM

2.1 Compensation.

