2.2 Inclusive Nature of Contract Sum. The Contract Sum stated in Section 2.1 is fully inclusive and comprehensive of all costs and expenses necessary to provide complete and finished Work in full compliance with the Contract Documents. The Contract Sum includes, without limitation: (a) all labor, including base wages, fringe benefits, workers' compensation, and payroll taxes for all workers; (b) all materials, equipment, and supplies incorporated into the Work or used in performance thereof; (c) all tools, equipment, and machinery required for installation and commissioning; (d) all freight, transportation, and delivery charges for materials and equipment; (e) all permits, licenses, inspections, and governmental fees; (f) all surety bonds and insurance required by this Agreement; (g) all testing, flushing, pressure testing, and system commissioning; (h) all temporary facilities, utilities, and accommodations necessary during construction; (i) all site cleanup, waste disposal, and final site restoration; (j) reasonable allocation of Contractor's overhead and profit; (k) contingency and risk allocation; and (l) all other costs whatsoever necessary to complete the Work in accordance with the Contract Documents. No additional compensation, reimbursement, or allowance shall be due to Contractor except as provided by properly executed and signed Change Orders issued by Owner or as specifically required elsewhere in this Agreement.

2.3 Sales and Use Taxes. Contractor shall be responsible for payment of all applicable California sales and use taxes on materials, equipment, and services incorporated into the Work. If Owner provides Contractor with evidence of a valid tax-exempt status or seller's permit exemption, and if Contractor is able to properly document the exemption, Contractor may request adjustment to the Contract Sum in the amount of documented taxes not paid. However, Contractor remains fully responsible for payment of all taxes that cannot be lawfully exempted. Contractor shall provide all required tax identification numbers, seller's permits, and resale certificates as necessary for proper tax compliance.

2.4 Prevailing Wage. To the extent that any portion of the Work is subject to prevailing wage requirements under California Labor Code Section 1773 or other applicable law, Contractor shall comply with all prevailing wage requirements and shall not be entitled to adjustment of the Contract Sum due to prevailing wage obligations. Contractor shall submit all required payroll records and prevailing wage documentation as required by law.

3. TIME FOR PERFORMANCE

3.1 Notice to Proceed and Commencement. Contractor shall not commence any work on the Project until receiving a written Notice to Proceed from Owner, signed by Owner's authorized representative. The anticipated Notice to Proceed date is June 2, 2025, but Owner reserves the right to advance or delay this date based on final Project readiness. Upon receipt of the Notice to Proceed, Contractor shall have five (5) business days to mobilize equipment and personnel and commence work. Time is of the essence in this Agreement, and Contractor acknowledges that delays in performance of the Work will cause substantial and demonstrable harm to Owner, including loss of expected building revenues, necessity for temporary cooling and water services, operational disruptions, and reputational harm. Contractor shall perform the Work diligently and continuously, without unreasonable delays.

3.2 Substantial Completion. Contractor shall achieve Substantial Completion of the Work by March 31, 2026. The term "Substantial Completion" means that the Work has been completed in all material respects in accordance with the Contract Documents, the chiller and cooling tower systems are operating and providing cooling and condenser water flow to the building, the domestic water systems are operating and providing hot and cold water throughout the building, all pressure testing has been completed and documented, all systems have been commissioned and balanced, and Owner can occupy and operate Capitol Tower in accordance with its normal business purposes. Substantial Completion does not require that every detail of work be finished, minor punch list items completed, or all cleaning finished, provided that such remaining items do not materially interfere with Owner's use of the building or systems. The date of Substantial Completion shall be established by written agreement of the parties following Contractor's written notice that the Work is substantially complete, or if the parties cannot agree within ten (10) days, by determination of an independent inspector selected jointly by the parties, with the cost of inspection split equally.

3.3 Final Completion. Within fourteen (14) days after Substantial Completion, Contractor shall complete all remaining punch list items, final cleaning and site restoration, correction of any deficient work identified during final inspection, and all other matters necessary to achieve Final Completion. "Final Completion" means that all work is finished, all punch list items are corrected, all documentation is provided, the site is cleaned and restored, and Owner has accepted the Work. Contractor shall cooperate with Owner in developing the final punch list and shall promptly complete all items within the 14-day period. If Contractor fails to complete punch list items within this period, Owner may hire others to complete such items and deduct costs from Contractor's final payment.

3.4 Liquidated Damages for Delay. Contractor acknowledges that failure to achieve Substantial Completion by the deadline specified in Section 3.2 will result in substantial and difficult-to-quantify damages to Owner. These damages include loss of expected lease revenues, increased operating costs for temporary cooling and water services, potential tenant dissatisfaction, operational disruptions, and other consequential losses. Because actual damages are difficult to ascertain in advance, the parties agree that Contractor shall pay liquidated damages to Owner in the amount of Two Thousand Five Hundred Dollars ($2,500.00) per calendar day for each day of delay beyond the Substantial Completion date, until the date of actual Substantial Completion. Such liquidated damages shall accumulate daily and shall be either deducted from Contractor's subsequent payments due, or if no further payments are due, Owner may pursue collection through legal action. The parties agree that these liquidated damages represent a reasonable pre-estimate of probable harm and are not intended as a penalty. Nothing in this section shall limit or waive Owner's right to pursue additional remedies available at law or in equity to recover damages in excess of the liquidated damages amount, nor shall payment of liquidated damages constitute acceptance of late completion.

3.5 No Assessment of Damages for Timely Completion. If Contractor achieves Substantial Completion on or before the date specified in Section 3.2, Owner shall pay Contractor in full without any discount, holdback, or penalty.

3.6 Time Extensions for Excusable Delays. If the Work is delayed by causes beyond Contractor's reasonable control, Contractor may request a time extension. Excusable causes include: (a) acts of God or natural disasters not reasonably foreseeable or preventable; (b) strikes or labor lockouts not involving Contractor's workforce; (c) governmental actions or legal injunctions; (d) pandemics or epidemics declared by governmental authorities; (e) unusually severe or unanticipated weather conditions not consistent with historical norms for Sacramento in the March through April 2026 timeframe; and (f) delays by other contractors or utility companies beyond Contractor's control. Contractor must provide written notice of the delay event to Owner within five (5) business days of learning of the delay, with detailed documentation of the cause, expected duration, impact on the critical path, and mitigation measures taken. Contractor must demonstrate that the delay is beyond its reasonable control and that it has taken reasonable steps to minimize the impact. Owner shall not unreasonably withhold approval of time extensions for qualifying delays. Approved time extensions shall be incorporated into an amended schedule and shall adjust the Substantial Completion date accordingly. Contractor's failure to timely provide notice of a delay shall result in forfeiture of the time extension claim.

3.7 Acceleration. If the Work falls behind schedule due to causes for which Contractor is responsible, or if Owner requests that Contractor accelerate performance to meet the Substantial Completion deadline, Contractor may submit a written proposal for acceleration costs within five (5) business days of the request or delay event. Acceleration costs may include documented overtime labor costs (at time-and-one-half), expedited delivery charges, additional equipment rental fees, and other direct costs caused by acceleration. All acceleration costs shall be supported by detailed documentation and shall be processed as a Change Order with Owner's written approval before Contractor incurs the costs. Owner shall not be obligated to approve acceleration requests or to pay acceleration costs, except by written Change Order.

4. PAYMENT AND RETAINAGE

4.1 Payment Schedule and Applications for Payment. Contractor may submit Applications for Payment on a monthly basis or at such other intervals as Owner may approve. Each Application for Payment shall be submitted in writing and shall include: (a) Contractor's detailed invoice describing all Work performed and costs incurred during the period; (b) a statement of the percentage of Work complete; (c) a summary of costs paid to subcontractors and material suppliers; (d) copies of significant supplier invoices if requested by Owner; (e) evidence of payment of all workers and suppliers; and (f) a sworn certification by Contractor that all statements are accurate and truthful. Applications for Payment shall be submitted by the 25th day of each month for work performed through the preceding month. Owner shall review and verify each Application for Payment and shall make payment of undisputed amounts within thirty (30) days of receipt of a complete application. Disputed amounts shall be identified in writing by Owner within the 30-day period, with Owner's specific reasons for the dispute.

4.2 Retainage and Holdback. Owner shall retain five percent (5%) of each monthly payment (including the value of incorporated materials and equipment) pending Final Completion of the Work. Retained funds shall not bear interest and shall not be subject to financing charges. Retainage shall be held in a separate account and shall not be commingled with Owner's general operating funds. Upon Final Completion of the Work and final inspection and acceptance by Owner, or upon agreement of the parties on completion status, the full retainage balance shall be paid to Contractor within thirty (30) days. If the parties dispute whether the Work is finally complete, an independent third-party inspector selected by mutual agreement shall make the determination within ten (10) days of the dispute, and the cost of such inspection shall be split equally between the parties. Upon the inspector's determination of Final Completion, the retainage shall be paid within 10 days.

4.3 Payment Conditions. Prior to final payment of the retainage, Contractor shall provide Owner with the following:

(a) A final, unconditional Waiver and Release of Lien covering all work performed through Final Completion;

(b) Evidence of payment in full of all suppliers, subcontractors, and workers;

(c) Updated Certificates of Insurance showing continued coverage in full force;

(d) Manufacturers' warranties transferred to Owner with evidence of registration;

(e) As-built drawings prepared by Contractor showing accurate routing of all piping, location of all equipment, and all actual dimensions and connections;

(f) A comprehensive Commissioning Report documenting all testing, startup procedures, system balancing results, performance verification, and training provided to Owner's personnel;

(g) Operations and maintenance manuals for all new equipment; and

(h) Copies of all inspection reports and permits indicating final approval by applicable authorities.

4.4 Right to Suspend Work for Non-Payment. If Owner fails to make any payment due to Contractor within forty-five (45) days after the payment due date, and Owner has not disputed the amount in good faith with reasonable documentation, Contractor may provide written notice to Owner that work will be suspended. If payment is not received within five (5) business days of such notice, Contractor may suspend work without liability for delay. During any suspension period, Contractor shall maintain the site in a safe condition and shall protect all equipment and materials. Contractor shall adjust the Substantial Completion date to add the full period of suspension plus seven (7) additional days to account for remobilization and recovery of productivity. Owner shall be liable for all costs of shutdown, storage, and remobilization, including demobilization costs of $15,000 and remobilization costs of $15,000.

4.5 No Financing or Interest Charges. Contractor shall not charge Owner financing interest or carrying charges on unpaid invoices unless specifically approved in advance in writing by Owner. However, if any payment remains unpaid for more than sixty (60) days after the due date and Contractor has suspended work as provided in Section 4.4, Contractor shall be entitled to recover reasonable financing costs and carrying costs, which shall not exceed one and one-half percent (1.5%) per month on the overdue balance, compounded monthly.

4.6 No Setoff or Offset. Owner shall not offset against payment amounts any claims Owner may have against Contractor or any disputes regarding quality or scope of work, unless Owner provides written notice of the specific dispute within ten (10) days of Contractor's submission of the Application for Payment. If Owner believes an Application for Payment is incorrect, Owner shall identify the specific items in dispute and the reasons therefor.

4.7 Final Payment Completion. Payment of the final retainage constitutes full and final settlement of all amounts due to Contractor under this Agreement, except for warranty obligations and claims properly reserved in writing by Contractor prior to final payment. Contractor's acceptance and deposit of final payment shall not constitute a waiver of claims unless Contractor has executed a separate written release of claims.

5. CHANGES IN THE WORK

5.1 Changes Only by Change Order. No changes, additions, deletions, or modifications to the scope of the Work shall be recognized or paid unless authorized by a written Change Order executed by authorized representatives of both Owner and Contractor. Contractor shall not perform any work beyond the Contract Documents without a properly executed Change Order, and Owner shall have no obligation to compensate Contractor for unauthorized work. All requests for changes, whether made verbally, by email, by text message, or by informal letter, must be confirmed by a formal, written Change Order before work commences. Any work performed without a Change Order shall be at Contractor's sole risk and expense, and Contractor waives the right to compensation for such work.

5.2 Change Order Procedure.

(a) Change Order Submission. If Owner requests changes to the scope of the Work, Owner shall provide written description of the requested change, the reason for the change, and the desired schedule impact. Within five (5) business days of receiving the change request, Contractor shall submit a written Change Order proposal that includes: (i) a detailed description of the specific scope change; (ii) any impacts on the contract time, with detailed schedule analysis showing how the change affects the critical path and Substantial Completion date; (iii) identification of any materials affected and detailed quotes from suppliers; (iv) an itemized labor cost estimate based on Contractor's standard labor rates; (v) equipment costs and rental fees directly attributable to the change; (vi) an allocation of indirect costs and overhead proportionate to the change in scope; (vii) contingency allowance appropriate to the risk; (viii) Contractor's markup and profit; and (ix) the total change in the Contract Sum.

(b) Supporting Documentation. Contractor's Change Order proposal shall be accompanied by: (i) written quotations from material suppliers; (ii) documentation of labor hour estimates and wage rates; (iii) equipment rental rate quotes; (iv) revised schedule showing impact on completion date; and (v) any other supporting information reasonably requested by Owner.

(c) Owner Review and Approval. Owner shall review Contractor's Change Order proposal and shall respond within ten (10) business days by: (i) approving the Change Order in full; (ii) requesting clarification or modification; or (iii) rejecting the change. If Owner requests modification, Contractor and Owner shall meet and confer and attempt to reach agreement on the change amount within five (5) additional days. If the parties cannot agree on the change amount, Owner may reject the change, and Contractor shall not proceed with the requested work.

(d) Effective Date and Authorization. No work on an approved change shall commence until the Change Order has been fully executed by both parties and a copy has been provided to Contractor. The Change Order shall be effective as of the date last signed. Once executed, the Change Order shall be incorporated into the Contract Documents and shall modify the Contract Sum and/or Substantial Completion date accordingly.

(e) Time Extensions. Any Change Order that adds substantial scope to the Work shall include analysis of the schedule impact. If the change delays other critical path work or requires additional time to complete, Contractor shall be entitled to an appropriate time extension, which shall be specified in the Change Order. Time extensions granted for Owner-directed changes shall not be subject to liquidated damages provisions for any delay resulting solely from the change.

(f) Cardinal Changes. If the cumulative value of all Change Orders issued exceeds twenty-five percent (25%) of the original Contract Sum, Contractor shall have the right to request renegotiation of rates, overhead allocation, or schedule, and the parties shall negotiate in good faith. Additionally, Contractor may request an adjustment to the Substantial Completion date if the cumulative changes materially impact the planned schedule. The cumulative effect of changes shall be measured both in terms of scope additions and cost impacts.

5.3 Differing Site Conditions. If during performance of the Work, Contractor encounters subsurface, concealed, or latent conditions that are materially different from those indicated in the Contract Documents or reasonably to be inferred from the Drawings and Specifications, Contractor shall immediately notify Owner in writing. Contractor shall not proceed with work affected by the differing condition until Owner instructs Contractor how to proceed. Owner shall determine whether a change in the Contract Sum or schedule is warranted, and if so, shall issue a Change Order. Differing conditions covered by this provision include: (a) existing copper piping routing different from that shown on Drawings; (b) structural conditions affecting equipment installation; (c) building conditions affecting access or installation feasibility; (d) site utility conflicts; (e) contamination or hazardous materials discovered during work; and (f) other physical conditions materially different from Contract Documents.

5.4 Omissions and Ambiguities in Contract Documents. If during construction, Contractor discovers that the Contract Documents contain omissions, ambiguities, conflicts, or insufficient detail to properly execute the Work in accordance with the specifications and intent, Contractor shall immediately notify Owner in writing. Contractor shall propose a solution that complies with the intent of the specifications and building codes. Owner shall approve the proposed solution and issue a Change Order for any additional cost. Contractor shall not delay overall progress of the Work pending resolution of the omitted or ambiguous item; rather, Contractor shall proceed with other work and propose the solution concurrently.

6. CLAIMS FOR ADDITIONAL COMPENSATION

6.1 Notice of Claim Required. If Contractor believes it is entitled to additional compensation beyond the Contract Sum due to any event, circumstance, delay, interference, change, or action by Owner (including but not limited to differing site conditions, delays caused by Owner, inadequate Contract Documents, Owner-directed changes, failures by Owner to perform its obligations, or interference by other contractors), Contractor must provide written notice to Owner within twenty-one (21) days of the date Contractor first knew or should have known of the event or circumstance giving rise to the claim. Notice must be in writing and delivered to Owner's Project Manager at the address specified in Section 13.5. Failure to provide timely notice shall waive Contractor's right to pursue the claim, except where the event or condition is continuing or recurring, in which case Contractor must provide notice within twenty-one (21) days of the date it knew of the continuing impact.

6.2 Content and Detail of Claims. All claims for additional compensation must be submitted in writing and must include:

(a) A detailed and comprehensive description of the basis for the claim, including the facts and circumstances giving rise to the entitlement;

(b) Specific citation to the relevant provisions of the Contract Documents upon which the claim is based;

(c) An itemized cost breakdown including: (i) direct labor costs with hours, wage rates, and workers' compensation; (ii) material costs supported by invoices; (iii) equipment costs or rental fees; (iv) subcontractor costs; and (v) allocation of indirect costs;

(d) Detailed documentation and evidence of actual expenditures incurred, including copies of invoices, payroll records, and subcontractor agreements;

(e) A detailed chronological timeline or schedule analysis showing the relationship between the claimed event and impacts to the Work;

(f) Photographic evidence, correspondence, or other documentation supporting the claim;

(g) The total dollar amount of the claim with detailed calculation methodology; and

(h) Contractor's acknowledgment that the claim is made in good faith and is true and correct.

6.3 Owner's Response and Good Faith Negotiation. Within twenty (20) days of receiving a complete claim from Contractor, Owner shall respond by either: (a) approving the claim in full; (b) approving the claim in part, with explanation of the partial approval; or (c) denying the claim with detailed written reasons. If Owner disputes the claim in whole or in part, Owner shall specifically identify which elements are disputed and why. Within ten (10) days of Owner's response, the parties' representatives shall meet in person or by videoconference to discuss the claim and attempt to reach resolution through good faith negotiation. If the parties cannot resolve the claim within ten (10) days after such meeting, either party may pursue available remedies.

6.4 Reservation of Rights and Continuing Performance. Submission of a claim shall not waive Contractor's right to pursue all available remedies, including claims for delay damages, acceleration costs, or contract modifications. Contractor's continued performance of work shall not constitute a waiver of the claim or acceptance of Owner's position regarding the dispute. The parties may agree in writing that performance shall continue pending resolution of claims, and such continuation shall not be deemed a waiver. Contractor's acceptance of partial payment in response to a claim shall not constitute acceptance of the disputed portion unless Contractor executes a separate written release of the disputed amount.

6.5 Late Claims. Any claim for additional compensation submitted more than twenty-one (21) days after the triggering event shall be barred and waived, except that claims arising from continuing conditions shall be timely if notice is provided within twenty-one (21) days of learning of the continuing impact. This bar shall be strictly enforced.

7. INSURANCE AND BONDING

7.1 Performance and Payment Bond. Prior to the commencement of any work on the Project, and at Contractor's sole expense, Contractor shall obtain and furnish to Owner fully executed copies of a Performance and Payment Bond ("Bond") issued by a corporate surety rated by A.M. Best at least A- (Excellent rating). The Bond shall be in the full amount of the Contract Sum or greater and shall remain in effect through Final Completion and for one (1) year thereafter to cover warranty and punch list work. The Performance Bond portion shall guarantee that Contractor shall complete the Work in accordance with the Contract Documents. The Payment Bond portion shall guarantee that Contractor shall pay all labor, materials, equipment, and other charges incurred in performance of the Work. The surety shall be authorized to conduct business in the State of California and shall demonstrate at least ten (10) years of continuous operation as a surety. Contractor shall not commence any work until executed Bond documents have been received and reviewed by Owner. If any Bond is cancelled, not renewed, or allowed to lapse, Contractor shall immediately furnish a replacement Bond on identical terms and in the full amount. Contractor shall maintain Bonds continuously at Contractor's cost. If Contractor fails to maintain Bonds as required, Owner may procure bonds at Contractor's expense and shall deduct the cost from amounts due to Contractor.

7.2 Contractor's Insurance Coverage.

(a) Comprehensive General Liability. Contractor shall maintain, at Contractor's sole cost and expense, comprehensive general liability insurance covering bodily injury, property damage, personal injury, and products-completed operations. The policy shall provide minimum limits of coverage of Two Million Dollars ($2,000,000) per occurrence and Two Million Dollars ($2,000,000) general aggregate. The policy shall include the following:

    (i) Owner, Harborview Realty Partners LP, Harborview Capitol Tower LLC, and their respective officers, directors, employees, agents, and representatives shall be named as additional insureds;

    (ii) A waiver of subrogation in favor of Owner and all additional insureds;

    (iii) A contractual liability endorsement covering Contractor's indemnification obligations;

    (iv) Notice of cancellation, material change, or non-renewal to be provided to Owner at least thirty (30) days in advance;

    (v) Primary and non-contributory coverage status as respects Owner's insurance;

    (vi) Agreement that the policy is not subject to other insurance clauses that would limit coverage; and

    (vii) A 30-day cancellation clause.

(b) Workers' Compensation Insurance. Contractor shall maintain workers' compensation insurance as required by California Labor Code, with statutory limits for all employees and any workers hired by Contractor. Contractor shall provide evidence of coverage upon request. If Contractor's employees are not subject to coverage, Contractor shall provide alternative coverage acceptable to Owner.

(c) Commercial Automobile Liability. Contractor shall maintain commercial automobile liability insurance covering all vehicles used in connection with the Work, including hired and non-owned vehicles. The minimum combined single limit shall be One Million Dollars ($1,000,000) per accident. The policy shall name Owner as an additional insured.

(d) Equipment and Tools. Contractor shall maintain coverage on its tools, equipment, and temporary facilities, or shall bear the loss if such items are damaged or stolen. Owner's insurance shall not cover Contractor's equipment.

(e) Proof of Insurance. Contractor shall provide Owner with Certificates of Insurance on ACORD form or equivalent, evidencing all required coverage prior to commencing work. Certificates shall name Owner as an additional insured and shall provide for thirty (30) days' notice of cancellation or material change. Certificates shall be updated annually and upon renewal of any policy. Owner reserves the right to conduct audits and inspections of Contractor's insurance policies at any time.

(f) Insurance Not a Limitation. Maintenance of insurance shall not limit or restrict Contractor's indemnification obligations or liability under this Agreement. Contractor shall remain fully liable for all damage and injury regardless of insurance coverage. If insurance proceeds are insufficient to cover all losses, Contractor shall pay the balance.

(g) Claims-Made Policies. Contractor shall not use claims-made insurance policies, or if such policies are used, Contractor shall maintain tail coverage extending one (1) year beyond Final Completion and shall provide evidence of tail coverage to Owner.

7.3 Owner's Insurance. Owner shall maintain property insurance on the Capitol Tower building structure and shall name Contractor as an additional insured under the builder's risk policy to the extent such coverage is available. Owner shall not be required to insure Contractor's tools, equipment, temporary facilities, or materials unless specifically agreed in writing. Owner's insurance shall be primary over any insurance maintained by Contractor for property damage to the building structure itself.

7.4 Insurance Requirements Not Diminished. Contractor's obligation to maintain insurance as required in this Section 7 shall not be diminished by the fact that Owner carries insurance. Contractor shall maintain all required insurance independently.

8. INDEMNIFICATION

8.1 Contractor's Indemnification of Owner. Contractor shall indemnify, defend with counsel acceptable to Owner, and hold harmless Owner, Harborview Capitol Tower LLC, Harborview Realty Partners LP, and their respective officers, directors, employees, agents, and representatives (collectively, the "Owner Indemnitees") from and against all claims, damages, liabilities, costs, expenses, and attorney's fees arising out of or resulting from:

(a) Bodily injury, death, or damage to property caused by any act, error, omission, or negligence of Contractor or its employees, agents, subcontractors, or representatives;

(b) Contractor's material breach of any obligation, representation, or warranty under this Agreement;

(c) Contractor's failure or refusal to comply with applicable laws, codes, regulations, or standards in performing the Work;

(d) Third-party claims by labor, suppliers, or subcontractors for non-payment or non-performance by Contractor;

(e) Contractor's infringement or violation of any intellectual property rights, patents, or copyrights;

(f) Environmental contamination or release of hazardous materials caused by Contractor's operations;

